<onlyinclude>'''Semiconductor manufacturing yield''' tells us what share of the chips made in a manufacturing run pass a specified test. If a wafer contains 500 eligible chips and 450 pass wafer testing, the measured wafer-test die yield is 90%. That does not mean 90% of packaged chips will pass every later test.


== Why chips fail ==
Some dies contain electrical faults; others work but cannot meet a required speed or power limit. Defects may cluster near a wafer edge or appear after a particular processing problem. Manufacturers test chips and sort them into performance or quality groups (''bins'').

== Why it matters ==
Yield can be measured at wafer testing, after packaging or within a particular performance group; percentages from different stages are not directly interchangeable. Higher yield means more usable chips from the same wafer, generally lowering manufacturing cost per usable chip. Larger chip designs often expose more area to defects, but area alone does not determine yield.


